---
import ManualLayout from '../../layouts/ManualLayout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<ManualLayout slug="links-and-graph">
  <h2 id="wiki-links">Wiki-links</h2>
  <p>
    In Edit, type <code>[[</code> and an autocomplete list of notes appears; keep typing to filter,
    and click or tap a note to insert it. The link is written as plain Markdown text — no hidden
    identifiers — so the file stays portable.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>[[Note title]]</code> — link to a note.</li>
    <li><code>[[Note title#Heading]]</code> — link straight to a section. Headings autocomplete too.</li>
    <li><code>[[Note title|shown text]]</code> — link with different display text.</li>
    <li>
      <code>[[Folder/Note title]]</code> — name a note by its folder when two notes share a title.
      Autocomplete offers titles and aliases, so this one is typed by hand; any trailing part of the
      path works, and <code>Manual/Index</code> is enough to distinguish it from another{' '}
      <code>Index</code> elsewhere.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Clicking a link opens that note. If the note does not exist yet, following the link creates it,
    so you can write the link first and fill it in later. Links resolve by name, so moving a note
    between folders does not break a link written as its title or an alias. A link that spells out
    a folder follows the note only while that part of its path stays the same.
  </p>

  <h2 id="aliases">Aliases</h2>
  <p>
    A note can answer to more than one name. Add aliases in the note’s front matter (in the
    panel’s Properties view, or directly):
  </p>
  <pre
    class="my-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-card border border-line bg-card p-4 text-[13px] text-body"><code>---
aliases: [TextKit, TextKit 2]
---</code></pre>
  <p>
    Links, autocomplete, search and unlinked mentions all understand aliases, so{' '}
    <code>[[TextKit]]</code> reaches the note whatever its filename is.
  </p>

  <h2 id="backlinks">Backlinks, outgoing links and mentions</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Links</strong>, in the right panel, shows what touches the open note:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Outgoing links</strong> — notes this one links to.</li>
    <li><strong>Linked mentions</strong> — its backlinks: notes that link <em>to</em> this one.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Unlinked mentions</strong> — notes that mention this note’s title or an alias in plain
      text without linking. Each has a <strong>Link</strong> button, which turns a mention of the
      title into a proper wiki-link in one click.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Unlinked mentions are the quiet workhorse: they surface connections you made before you had a
    note for the idea.
  </p>

  <h2 id="transclusion">Transclusion</h2>
  <p>
    Embed one note inside another with <code>![[Note title]]</code>, or embed a single section with{' '}
    <code>![[Note title#Heading]]</code>. Embeds accept a folder path in the same way links do —{' '}
    <code>![[Manual/Index#Keyboard shortcuts]]</code>. The embedded content is rendered inline —
    the real thing, not a copy — which is useful for definitions, boilerplate or a running index.
  </p>

  <h2 id="graph">The Graph: one note’s links</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Graph</strong> is a view of the right panel, so it is about the note you have open:
    that note, picked out, with what links to it and what it links to as nodes and arrows. Open it with <strong>View ▸ Graph</strong> (<kbd>⇧⌘G</kbd>), or choose{' '}
    <strong>Graph</strong> at the top of the panel.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The pop-up beside <em>Links in and out</em> sets how far it reaches: <em>Direct links</em>, or{' '}
      <em>Within 2 links</em> or <em>Within 3 links</em> for what those notes connect to in turn.
    </li>
    <li>Click a node to trace its links: the arrows show which it links to and which link to it.</li>
    <li>Double-click a node to open that note. The graph follows you to it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because it is in the panel, the note stays beside it and you can keep writing with the graph
    open.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mind-map">The Mind Map: the whole collection</h2>
  <p>
    Where the Graph is one note’s links, the <strong>Mind Map</strong> is the links across the
    whole collection, drawn as a mind map. Open it from <strong>Mind Map</strong> at the top of the
    sidebar, or with <strong>View ▸ Mind Map</strong> (<kbd>⇧⌘M</kbd>); it opens as a tab beside
    your notes.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The collection is at the centre. Its best-connected notes head the coloured branches, and
      every other linked note sits on the branch that reaches it first.
    </li>
    <li>
      Links the branches leave out are drawn as faint dashed lines, so every link is on the map.
    </li>
    <li>
      Under the title it says how many notes and links it shows — and how many notes have no links
      at all, which is usually a note you forgot to link rather than one nobody needs.
    </li>
    <li>Click a note to open it. Choosing a note puts the map’s tab behind it, a click away.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On a very large collection the map keeps the 160 most-connected notes and says how many it
    left off.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Link generously while writing; the panel’s Links view makes the value visible later.</li>
    <li>Rename with <strong>Note ▸ Rename…</strong> so links across the collection are rewritten.</li>
    <li>
      Use <a href={href('/manual/organising')}>tags</a> for categories and links for relationships —
      they answer different questions.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ManualLayout>
